{"name":"Black Headed Gull","hex":"#9c856c","fetched":"2026-10-11","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"black","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Absorbing all light and reflecting none; dark and hueless.","Without light."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The colour/color perceived in the absence of light, but also when no light is reflected, but rather absorbed.","A black dye or pigment."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/black"},{"term":"headed","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Of a sheet of paper: having the sender's name, address, etc. preprinted at the top.","Having a head or brain with specified characteristics."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["simple past and past participle of head"]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/headed"},{"term":"gull","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A seabird of the genus Larus or of the subfamily Larinae.","Any of various pierid butterflies of the genus Cepora."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A cheating trick; a fraud.","A stupid animal."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/gull"}],"entries":[{"title":"Black-headed gull","blocks":[{"text":"The black-headed gull is a small gull that breeds in much of the Palearctic in Europe and Asia, and also locally in smaller numbers in coastal eastern Canada. Most of the population is migratory and winters further south, but many also remain in the milder areas of northwestern Europe. It was formerly sometimes cited as \"common black-headed gull\" to distinguish it from \"great black-headed gull\"."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/29/Chroicocephalus_ridibundus_%28summer%29.jpg/330px-Chroicocephalus_ridibundus_%28summer%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Black-headed_gull","wikidata":"Q25634"},{"title":"Gull","blocks":[{"text":"Gulls, informally called seagulls, are seabirds of the subfamily Larinae. They are most closely related to terns and skimmers, and are placed with them in the family Laridae. They are also related, more distantly, to auks and skuas, and more distantly still to waders. Until the 21st century, most gulls were placed in the genus Larus, but that arrangement is now considered polyphyletic, leading to the readoption and revision of several genera."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b9/Herring_Gull_%28Larus_argentatus%29%2C_Brighton%2C_England.JPG/330px-Herring_Gull_%28Larus_argentatus%29%2C_Brighton%2C_England.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gull","wikidata":"Q2699803"},{"title":"Black","blocks":[{"text":"Black is a color that results from the absence or complete absorption of visible light. It is an achromatic color, without chroma, like white and grey. It is often used symbolically or figuratively to represent darkness. Black and white have often been used to describe opposites such as good and evil, the Dark Ages versus the Age of Enlightenment, and night versus day. Since the Middle Ages, black has been the symbolic color of solemnity and authority, and for this reason it is still commonly worn by judges and magistrates."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/fc/Tutankhamun_jackal_%28blacked%29.png/330px-Tutankhamun_jackal_%28blacked%29.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Black","wikidata":"Q23445"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a9/Black-headed_gull_%2805557%29.jpg/500px-Black-headed_gull_%2805557%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a9/Black-headed_gull_%2805557%29.jpg/1280px-Black-headed_gull_%2805557%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"